\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Inverse rotations with identical path pairings}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{Take one edge with distinct endpoints and no faces. Let
#{C_3\times C_2} act on the equilateral orbit in the real plane
through rotations by #{C_3}, with the second factor acting trivially.
Use the Euclidean dot product for every path pairing and choose
the same unit reference vector at both endpoints. If
#{c} generates #{C_3}, compare the links #{(c,1)} and
#{(c^{-1},1)}. In the residual action of \ref{fdip-0112} each endpoint
stabilizer is #{\{1\}\times C_2}; multiplying at either end
preserves the first component. Hence the two links belong to
distinct residual orbits. The indicator of the first orbit
distinguishes them.}

\p{Every nonempty walk on this graph alternates a traversal and
its reverse. A closed walk cancels to the identity, and an open
walk reduces to the single traversal between its endpoints.
All [scalar path pairings](fdip-011B) of the closed walks are one.
For either orientation of an open walk they are
#{\cos(2\pi /3)=-1/2} for both configurations. There are no
plaquette holonomies to add. The complete list of these
measurements therefore fails to separate the two orbits even
for this Abelian group.}

\p{If the group is #{C_3} itself, the same scalar pairings
again coincide. Now the orbit is free and the group-valued
[framed path transport](fdip-011B) on the oriented edge
takes the distinct values #{c} and #{c^{-1}}, as predicted by
\ref{fdip-011C}.}
